\section{Background and Related Work}
\label{sec:background_related_work}

This section provides the foundational context necessary to understand the proposed scheduling architecture. It first outlines the evolution of cloud-native telecommunications and edge AI, before critically reviewing the existing literature on GPU sharing, edge multi-tenancy, and RAN-aware scheduling to highlight the specific gaps this research will address.

\subsection{Background}
\label{subsec:background}

\subsubsection{Cloud-Native O-RAN Architecture}
Historically, cellular network deployments relied heavily on monolithic, proprietary hardware appliances characterized by strict vendor lock-in and rigid operational paradigms. The Open RAN (O-RAN) standard initiated a fundamental shift in this ecosystem by promoting the disaggregation of traditional baseband functions and the adoption of standardized, open interfaces \cite{openairan,beyond,airan}. A central tenet of the O-RAN specification is the defined functional split—most notably Split 7.2—which cleanly divides Physical (PHY) layer processing \cite{openairan,beyond,airan}. Under this architecture, the lower PHY functions reside at the Radio Unit (O-RU), while the computationally intensive upper PHY operations are handled by the Distributed Unit (O-DU) \cite{openairan,beyond,airan}.

Crucially, O-RAN transitions these disaggregated components away from specialized telecommunications equipment toward a highly flexible, cloud-native architecture \cite{openairan,beyond,airan}. In a virtualized RAN (vRAN) deployment, network functions are completely decoupled from the underlying physical hardware and are instead deployed as containerized microservices \cite{openairan,beyond}. These virtualized workloads run on Commercial Off-The-Shelf (COTS) servers, which collectively form the computing substrate known as the O-Cloud. To manage this distributed environment, the architecture introduces the Service Management and Orchestration (SMO) framework \cite{beyond,airan}. The SMO leverages standard IT cloud orchestration principles to handle the lifecycle management, dynamic scaling, and deployment of virtualized network functions across the O-Cloud, ensuring multi-vendor interoperability and programmatic control \cite{beyond,airan}. Furthermore, O-RAN integrates AI/ML-driven network automation into the architecture, enabling intelligent orchestration of both computational and communication resources \cite{airan,beyond}.

However, realizing this cloud-native flexibility introduces severe execution challenges, primarily due to the stringent, deterministic nature of cellular telecommunications. RAN functions demand substantial compute and network resources. The network operates in a strict time-slotted fashion, with slot durations ranging from hundreds of microseconds to just one millisecond \cite{chronos}. During a single slot, the system must execute radio resource allocation in the control plane and process user packets in the data plane (e.g., segmentation, header attachment, and retransmissions) \cite{chronos}.

Executing these slot-level tasks on standard cloud infrastructure is demanding. CHRONOS shows that VM interruption and network latency can consume a large share of the slot budget, so faithful cloud execution depends on tight release synchrony and careful control of timing jitter \cite{chronos}. This timing pressure helps explain why recent O-RAN and AI-RAN work places disaggregated DU/CU software, O-Cloud resources, and accelerator support within a common cloud-native operating model, rather than treating real-time RAN functions as fixed-purpose appliances \cite{openairan,beyond,airan}.


\subsubsection{The AI-RAN Paradigm}
As cellular systems move toward 6G, the RAN is increasingly framed as both a communications system and a distributed compute platform. The AI-RAN literature describes this convergence as a joint evolution of infrastructure, control, and services, where AI capabilities are embedded into the RAN stack and coordinated across edge and cloud resources \cite{beyond,airan,openairan}.

The recent literature organizes this convergence into three closely related pillars \cite{airan,openairan}:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{AI-for-RAN (Network Optimization):} This pillar applies AI to improve internal network functions, including scheduling, beam management, traffic steering, energy saving, and anomaly handling \cite{airan,openairan}. Here, AI remains subordinate to the primary telecommunications service.
	\item \textbf{AI-on-RAN (Native AI Services):} This pillar treats the RAN and its edge compute continuum as a native platform for AI services, especially distributed learning and low-latency inference placed close to DU/CU resources \cite{airan}.
	\item \textbf{AI-and-RAN (Infrastructure Multiplexing):} This pillar asks when spare O-Cloud or AI-RAN compute can host secondary AI workloads without breaking primary radio deadlines \cite{airan,weaver,beyond}.
\end{itemize}

This research proposal focuses strictly on the \textbf{AI-and-RAN} pillar. Relative to the broader AI-for-RAN literature, it remains at an earlier stage of system design and validation, even though it offers a clear economic reason to reuse AI-RAN infrastructure beyond peak radio demand periods \cite{airan,weaver,beyond}.

In practice, that reuse depends on turning spare DU or O-Cloud compute into a controlled edge service substrate while preserving the primacy of RAN timing and reliability requirements \cite{beyond,weaver,chronos}. The central systems question is therefore not whether AI workloads can be injected in the abstract, but how to admit and coordinate only those workloads that remain compatible with the underlying radio deadlines \cite{airan,weaver}.


\subsubsection{The Anatomy of Latency-Critical Inference Workloads}
Latency-sensitive edge AI workloads are also part of this background. CORA models their end-to-end service time as a joint uplink, compute, and downlink problem, while recent device-RAN-cloud measurements show that strict edge feasibility depends on workload variant and placement rather than on accelerator presence alone \cite{cora,slaairan}.

As a result, edge inference is a multi-resource scheduling problem whose bottleneck can shift with channel quality, traffic load, and input/output size \cite{cora}. For generative services, this difficulty is amplified by the fact that different stages of inference place different demands on the platform \cite{slaairan}.

The first stage is the \textbf{Prefill Phase} (or prompt processing). In this stage, the model processes the prompt in parallel and establishes the time-to-first-token budget. This phase is typically more compute-heavy than later generation, which is why TTFT becomes a useful operational signal under strict edge SLAs \cite{slaairan}.

The second stage is the \textbf{Autoregressive Decoding Phase}. Modern serving systems consistently distinguish it from prefill: prefill is prompt-parallel and typically compute-dense, so it dominates time-to-first-token, whereas decoding is token-serial and advances one token at a time \cite{slaairan,sarathiserve,distserve}. Because each decode step must reuse the accumulated KV cache while doing comparatively little arithmetic, its bottleneck is usually memory movement and KV-cache management rather than raw ALU saturation \cite{pagedattention,sarathiserve}. This is why steady per-token latency or throughput behaves differently from TTFT under strict edge SLAs \cite{slaairan}.

This phase split is also visible in the design of current serving engines. PagedAttention/vLLM shows that the KV cache is large, dynamically sized, and easily wasted by fragmentation or duplication, so explicit KV-cache memory management directly affects feasible batch size and serving throughput \cite{pagedattention}. Sarathi-Serve shows that although batching decodes is throughput-efficient, naive interleaving of prefills and decodes creates interference; its chunked-prefill scheduler is designed to admit new requests without stalling ongoing decodes \cite{sarathiserve}. DistServe goes further and shows that under tight TTFT and per-token latency constraints, colocating both phases can couple their resource-allocation plans badly enough that some systems explicitly disaggregate prefill and decoding onto different GPUs \cite{distserve}.

For AI-RAN, this means GPU ``spare capacity'' cannot be inferred from utilization counters alone. During decoding, low SM or ALU utilization can coexist with high pressure on HBM, caches, and KV-state traffic, so a scheduler that interprets low compute utilization as safe headroom can admit RAN work precisely when contention risk remains high \cite{pagedattention,sarathiserve,distserve}. CORA and recent device--RAN--cloud evidence reinforce that this risk is end-to-end: feasibility depends on model variant, placement, channel quality, and queueing across the uplink--compute--downlink path, not on accelerator presence alone \cite{cora,slaairan}.


\subsubsection{Strict RAN Service Level Agreements (SLAs) vs. IT Workloads}
Sharing this cloud-native hardware introduces a real clash of scheduling objectives. In standard IT clouds, schedulers can chase fairness or average utilization. In the RAN, CHRONOS shows that VM interruption and network latency can already consume a large fraction of the slot budget, so release synchrony and timing jitter are first-order constraints rather than implementation details \cite{chronos}. Any coexistence policy for DU-adjacent GPUs therefore has to preserve a strict ``RAN-first'' priority model instead of a best-effort cloud policy \cite{chronos,weaver}.


\subsubsection{The Hardware Dichotomy: Training vs. Inference}
Within the AI domain, the workload type matters as much as the accelerator itself. Weaver shows why foundation-model training is the friendlier co-located case: LDPC-heavy RAN processing is largely bandwidth-dominated, while training is more compute-heavy and elastic, so spare SM envelopes can be harvested for secondary work without assuming the same resource profile as the radio path \cite{weaver}. By contrast, CORA and recent device, RAN, and cloud measurements frame inference as an interactive end-to-end service whose feasibility depends on model variant, placement, channel quality, and deadline budget rather than on GPU presence alone \cite{cora,slaairan}. A scheduler that works for elastic training therefore cannot be carried over directly to latency-critical inference \cite{weaver,cora,slaairan}.


\subsection{Related Work}
\label{subsec:related_work}

\subsubsection{Hardware-Level GPU Sharing Mechanisms}
To realize AI-and-RAN, the GPU must be shared between the RAN DU and the inference stack, but the mechanism choices mostly expose a trade-off rather than a complete answer. Spatial partitioning such as MIG offers strong isolation and is therefore a useful baseline when strict SLA classes need dedicated slices, yet static partitions are a poor fit for bursty spare compute and can strand capacity that arrives at slot timescales \cite{weaver,slaairan}. More elastic temporal sharing can expose more headroom, but Weaver treats mechanism overhead and sharing jitter as central concerns for RAN-safe reuse \cite{weaver}. These primitives matter, but they do not by themselves provide an inference-aware micro-scheduling policy for a shared DU GPU \cite{weaver,slaairan}.

\subsubsection{Edge Multi-Tenancy and Orchestration}
Providing AI inference as a multi-tenant service also requires a slow-timescale orchestration layer. Paradrop is a useful precedent here because it shows that lightweight, containerized services can be deployed at the network's extreme edge with backend orchestration and explicit resource controls \cite{paradrop}. That said, Paradrop targets lightweight edge services on constrained nodes, while AI-RAN proposals extend orchestration toward the O-Cloud and SMO stack for joint RAN and AI resource management \cite{paradrop,beyond}. The orchestration lesson is valuable, but it still has to be paired with a fast local execution policy for shared accelerators.

\subsubsection{End-to-End Latency Coordination}
Inference latency spans uplink transport, edge execution, and downlink return, and CORA is the strongest direct template in this corpus for coordinating those stages \cite{cora}. CORA explicitly budgets latency across uplink, compute, and downlink, derives RB demand from I/O size and channel quality, derives SM demand from a speedup model, and performs pre-admission costing with lightweight planner overhead of about 0.18 ms and roughly 112 B of metadata per request \cite{cora}. This is strong evidence that end-to-end latency budgeting is feasible, but CORA still treats compute as a schedulable stage budget rather than as a shared RAN-GPU micro-scheduling problem \cite{cora,slaairan}.

\subsubsection{RAN-Aware Co-scheduling}
Weaver comes closest to the local sharing problem because it embeds a RAN-first spare-compute controller into the MAC scheduler and exports slot-bounded GPU headroom for secondary AI work \cite{weaver}. Its profiling is concrete: even a pessimistic four-DU peak-load setup still leaves about 40\% spare SM capacity, and production-trace replays show spare capacity can remain around 85\% during peak daytime periods, although that headroom is bursty within sites and complementary across sites \cite{weaver}. However, Weaver's target workload is foundation-model training, not interactive inference, and the same evidence base warns that LLM decoding is also bandwidth-heavy, which makes it a weaker sharing match with LDPC than training is \cite{weaver,slaairan}.


\subsection{Summary of Research Gap}
\label{subsec:research_gap}
Taken together, the literature covers the surrounding layers but not the core inference-sharing problem. CHRONOS explains why slot-realistic timing leaves little slack for interference, while Paradrop supplies a pattern for multi-tenant edge orchestration \cite{chronos,paradrop}. CORA supplies end-to-end latency budgeting, and Weaver shows how compatible secondary workloads can harvest spare RAN GPU capacity \cite{cora,weaver}. What is still missing is an inference-specific micro-scheduler for shared RAN GPUs, one that reasons about phase-sensitive inference behavior and admits only those requests whose latency and resource profile remain compatible with slot-level DU deadlines \cite{weaver,cora,slaairan}.


%==========================================================
% Chapter 3 -> Section 3
\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}

To test the core hypothesis and develop the proposed multi-tenant AI-RAN architecture, this research will adopt a phased, systems-engineering methodology.

\textbf{Phase 1: Workload Profiling and Contention Analysis.}\\
Before a scheduler can be designed, the exact resource demands of both workloads must be established. We will profile standard 5G DU baseband processing alongside a suite of edge AI models (LLMs/VLMs) to establish their precise compute-bound versus memory-bound characteristics. Co-locating these workloads will identify exactly which hardware resources (L2 Cache, memory bandwidth, PCIe bus) cause RAN SLA violations.

\textbf{Phase 2: Micro-Level Scheduler Design.}\\
Based on profiling data, a dynamic MAC-layer scheduler will be developed. Instead of relying on static partitioning, it will adapt NVIDIA Green Contexts and sub-millisecond preemption protocols to safely inject memory-bound inference tasks into RAN idle slots without compromising the primary telecommunication deadline.

\textbf{Phase 3: Macro-Level Orchestration Integration.}\\
The scheduler will be integrated into a broader cloud-native framework to achieve multi-tenancy. This involves extending standard orchestration principles (such as Kubernetes pods or Paradrop-style isolated environments) to handle tenant isolation and implement the decoupled end-to-end latency planning mechanism.


